16
00:01:08.061 --> 00:01:09.981
Introduction and Why It Matters.

17
00:01:11.231 --> 00:01:14.537
A story: the resharding that moved 80 % of the data.

18
00:01:14.817 --> 00:01:20.492
By the second Diwali sale, the orders database had outgrown one PostgreSQL primary.

19
00:01:20.772 --> 00:01:28.217
The team split it into four shards with the simplest rule anyone could think of: shard = hash(customerid) % 4.

20
00:01:28.497 --> 00:01:29.607
It worked for a year.

21
00:01:30.957 --> 00:01:32.919
Then growth demanded a fifth shard.

22
00:01:33.199 --> 00:01:41.434
The change to the rule was one character, % 4 became % 5, and the migration plan said copy rows whose shard changed.

23
00:01:41.714 --> 00:01:43.997
Nobody had calculated how many that was.

24
00:01:44.277 --> 00:01:46.858
About 80 % of all rows changed shards.

25
00:01:47.138 --> 00:01:58.253
The copy ran for 31 hours, ate the disk and I or O budget of all five databases, and made p99 checkout latency four times worse for the whole weekend.

26
00:01:58.533 --> 00:02:01.071
Worse, writes kept arriving during the copy.

27
00:02:01.351 --> 00:02:08.839
Rows written to the old shard after they had been copied, but before the application switched rules, were simply not on the new shard.

28
00:02:09.119 --> 00:02:17.525
212 orders disappeared from customers' order history until a reconciliation script found them in the old shards a week later.

29
00:02:18.875 --> 00:02:29.136
Two months later a different incident: the new mega dark store in Bengaluru, 5 % of all orders by itself, landed on the same shard as three other busy stores.

30
00:02:29.416 --> 00:02:36.349
The store dashboard, which queried orders by storeid, pinned that shard's CPU at 95 % every evening.

31
00:02:36.629 --> 00:02:39.424
Neither incident was about the database engine.

32
00:02:39.704 --> 00:02:41.859
Both were partitioning design mistakes:

33
00:02:43.209 --> 00:02:46.985
The key, then shard function must not depend on the number of shards.

34
00:02:47.265 --> 00:02:55.094
Hash into a fixed number of logical buckets (1,024 here), and map buckets to shards in a small, versioned table.

35
00:02:55.374 --> 00:02:59.854
Adding a shard then moves ~1 or N of the data, a bucket at a time.

36
00:03:00.134 --> 00:03:01.478
A move needs a fence.

37
00:03:01.758 --> 00:03:08.691
Copy while writable, then block writes to the bucket on the source for a short final delta, verify, flip the map.

38
00:03:08.971 --> 00:03:14.689
A stale writer gets BUCKETMOVED and retries, instead of writing into a copy nobody will read.

39
00:03:16.039 --> 00:03:20.497
Pick the partition key from the dominant access pattern, and plan for the others.

40
00:03:20.777 --> 00:03:31.871
Orders are read by customer (partition by customer) but also by store (a separately partitioned read model or a bounded scatter-gather), and a single huge key needs splitting.

41
00:03:33.221 --> 00:03:33.903
Quote:

42
00:03:34.183 --> 00:03:35.847
The thesis of this chapter.

43
00:03:36.127 --> 00:03:41.311
Partitioning buys capacity by giving up the ability to see all the data in one place.

44
00:03:41.591 --> 00:03:49.783
Every design is a choice of which queries stay on one partition, which ones fan out, and how data moves when the partition count changes.

45
00:03:50.063 --> 00:03:53.839
Make those three choices on purpose, before the data forces them.

