117
00:06:52.626 --> 00:06:53.501
Part 2.

118
00:06:53.781 --> 00:06:55.658
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

119
00:06:56.908 --> 00:06:59.383
Intuition: the post office sorting room.

120
00:06:59.663 --> 00:07:07.087
One clerk sorting all of a city's mail can't keep up, so the post office hires eight clerks and gives each one a set of pigeonholes.

121
00:07:07.367 --> 00:07:21.191
If clerks split the mail by pincode range (560001 to 560020 to clerk 1, and so on), finding all mail for 5600xx is easy: one clerk.

122
00:07:21.471 --> 00:07:28.084
But the new IT park in 560103 floods one clerk while others are idle.

123
00:07:28.364 --> 00:07:30.881
That's range partitioning and its hot range.

124
00:07:31.161 --> 00:07:41.465
If clerks split the mail by a shuffle of the pincode (a hash), the work spreads evenly, but all mail for 5600xx now means asking all eight.

125
00:07:41.745 --> 00:07:43.836
That's hash partitioning and scatter-gather.

126
00:07:45.186 --> 00:07:52.610
If the post office numbers clerks and uses pincode % 8, hiring a ninth clerk reshuffles almost every pigeonhole.

127
00:07:52.890 --> 00:08:05.711
If instead it has 1,024 labelled pigeonholes and gives each clerk a set of them, hiring a ninth clerk means handing over about 1 or 9 of the pigeonholes, and the labels on the letters never change.

128
00:08:05.991 --> 00:08:07.442
That's logical buckets.

129
00:08:07.722 --> 00:08:13.781
Replication (Ch 10) is photocopying a clerk's pigeonholes so a second clerk can take over.

130
00:08:14.061 --> 00:08:16.365
Partitioning is splitting the pigeonholes.

131
00:08:16.645 --> 00:08:20.143
Real systems do both: each partition is replicated.

132
00:08:21.493 --> 00:08:24.352
Partitioning versus replication versus sharding.

133
00:08:24.632 --> 00:08:25.976
Here is a quick table:

134
00:08:26.256 --> 00:08:27.408
Term, and Meaning.

135
00:08:27.688 --> 00:08:28.648
Partitioning:

136
00:08:28.928 --> 00:08:32.341
Splitting one dataset into disjoint pieces (partitions).

137
00:08:32.621 --> 00:08:39.171
Can happen inside one database server (PostgreSQL declarative partitioning) or across servers.

138
00:08:39.451 --> 00:08:40.283
Sharding:

139
00:08:40.563 --> 00:08:47.197
Partitioning across servers, so each server (shard) holds only some of the data and takes only some of the writes.

140
00:08:47.477 --> 00:08:48.501
Replication:

141
00:08:48.781 --> 00:08:52.067
Copying the same data to several servers (Ch 10).

142
00:08:52.347 --> 00:08:53.115
Shard:

143
00:08:53.395 --> 00:08:59.453
One physical database cluster (a primary + replicas) holding a set of partitions or buckets.

144
00:08:59.733 --> 00:09:01.568
Partition key or shard key:

145
00:09:01.848 --> 00:09:05.155
The column(s) whose value decides where a row lives.

146
00:09:06.505 --> 00:09:09.491
Replication scales reads and survives failures.

147
00:09:09.771 --> 00:09:14.571
It doesn't scale writes or storage: every replica stores and applies every write.

148
00:09:14.851 --> 00:09:19.331
Partitioning is the only way to scale writes and storage beyond one machine.

149
00:09:19.611 --> 00:09:24.582
That's why every large system does both, with names like shard 3, replica 2.

150
00:09:24.862 --> 00:09:30.814
Partition inside one server first (it's cheap, and it helps with pruning, maintenance and retention).

151
00:09:31.094 --> 00:09:43.105
Shard across servers only when one primary can't take the writes or hold the data, after the Ch 09 steps (indexes, caching, read replicas, a bigger machine) are exhausted.

152
00:09:44.455 --> 00:09:45.735
Range partitioning.

153
00:09:46.015 --> 00:09:57.129
Each partition holds a contiguous range of keys: orders by month, customers A, F, pincodes 560001 to 560050.

154
00:09:58.479 --> 00:09:59.823
Here is a quick table:

155
00:10:00.103 --> 00:10:01.575
Strengths, and Weaknesses.

156
00:10:01.855 --> 00:10:05.930
Range scans stay in one or a few partitions (orders in September):

157
00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:18.071
Monotonic keys (time, sequence ids) send all new writes to the newest partition (L1 D: all 10,000 of today's writes land in 2026 to 09).

158
00:10:18.351 --> 00:10:25.306
Dropping old data is DROP TABLE orders202501 (instant, no vacuum):

159
00:10:25.586 --> 00:10:30.215
Skewed key distributions make uneven partitions; boundaries need tuning.

160
00:10:30.495 --> 00:10:33.333
Split a big range into two without touching others:

161
00:10:33.613 --> 00:10:36.130
Needs a range, then partition directory.

162
00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:45.373
Good fit: time-series retention inside one server, where all writes in the newest partition is fine because it's still one server.

163
00:10:45.653 --> 00:10:55.531
Used across servers by HBase, Bigtable, Spanner, CockroachDB and TiDB, which automatically split ranges that get too big or too hot.

164
00:10:56.881 --> 00:11:00.187
Hash partitioning. partition = f(hash(key)).

165
00:11:00.467 --> 00:11:08.659
A good hash spreads keys evenly, whatever the key distribution (L1 A: max or avg 1.00x).

166
00:11:10.009 --> 00:11:11.353
Here is a quick table:

167
00:11:11.633 --> 00:11:13.105
Strengths, and Weaknesses.

168
00:11:13.385 --> 00:11:17.396
Even spread of data and writes, no hot range from monotonic keys:

169
00:11:17.676 --> 00:11:19.831
Range queries touch every partition.

170
00:11:20.111 --> 00:11:22.777
No directory needed if f is a formula:

171
00:11:23.057 --> 00:11:28.924
Every query without the key touches every partition (lab A: storeid scanned all 8 partitions). :

172
00:11:29.204 --> 00:11:34.687
One very hot key still lands on one partition (hashing spreads keys, not load per key).

173
00:11:36.037 --> 00:11:40.026
The hash must be stable across languages, versions and processes.

174
00:11:40.306 --> 00:11:49.287
Java's String.hashCode() happens to be specified, but Object.hashCode() isn't, and many languages randomize string hashes per process.

175
00:11:49.567 --> 00:11:59.615
Use a named hash (CRC32C, Murmur3, xxHash, or MD5 when speed doesn't matter) and pin it with a golden-value test:

176
00:11:59.895 --> 00:12:04.887
Buckets.of(cust-42) == 532 must hold forever.

177
00:12:06.237 --> 00:12:10.035
From hash mod N to consistent hashing to logical buckets.

178
00:12:10.315 --> 00:12:12.960
The question is what happens when N changes.

179
00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:18.168
L1 measured it with 200,000 customers going from 4 to 5 shards:

180
00:12:19.518 --> 00:12:40.574
On screen: scheme max or avg moved on 4, then 5. hash mod N 1.00x 80.1%. consistent hashing, 1 point per shard 1.80x 17.2%. consistent hashing, 150 vnodes 1.02x 20.3%.

181
00:12:40.854 --> 00:12:47.510
1,024 fixed buckets, then shards 1.00x 19.7%.

182
00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:51.469
hash mod N.

183
00:12:51.749 --> 00:13:05.893
Perfectly even, but changing N remaps a key unless h mod 4 == h mod 5, which is true for only ~1 or 5 of keys. ~80 % of data moves (the §1.1 story).

184
00:13:06.173 --> 00:13:14.259
Only acceptable if N never changes, or if you always double N (then exactly half the keys move, and each shard splits into two).

185
00:13:15.609 --> 00:13:17.806
Consistent hashing (Karger et al.

186
00:13:18.086 --> 00:13:19.707
1997).

187
00:13:19.987 --> 00:13:25.171
Hash both keys and shards onto a ring, and a key belongs to the next shard clockwise.

188
00:13:25.451 --> 00:13:30.977
Adding a shard takes keys only from its clockwise neighbour, so only ~1 or N moves.

189
00:13:31.257 --> 00:13:41.646
With one point per shard the arcs are uneven (1.80x: one shard has almost twice the average), and the new shard takes from one neighbour, not from all.

190
00:13:41.926 --> 00:13:51.206
Virtual nodes (150 points per shard) fix both: even load (1.02x), and a new shard takes a little from everyone.

191
00:13:51.486 --> 00:13:56.115
Used by Dynamo, Cassandra (vnodes or tokens), Riak, many caches.

192
00:13:57.465 --> 00:13:59.428
Fixed logical buckets (slots).

193
00:13:59.708 --> 00:14:11.079
Hash keys into a fixed number of buckets B that never changes (1,024, or Redis Cluster's 16,384), and keep a bucket, then shard map.

194
00:14:11.359 --> 00:14:15.220
Adding a shard means reassigning ~B or N buckets to it:

195
00:14:15.500 --> 00:14:18.572
19.7 % moved, perfectly even.

196
00:14:18.852 --> 00:14:23.588
It's consistent hashing with the ring replaced by an explicit table, which gives you:

197
00:14:23.868 --> 00:14:29.201
Control: move exactly the buckets you choose, one at a time, verifiable, pausable.

198
00:14:29.481 --> 00:14:32.404
Unequal shards: give a bigger machine more buckets.

199
00:14:32.684 --> 00:14:36.567
Hot bucket isolation: move one hot bucket to its own shard.

200
00:14:37.917 --> 00:14:44.210
A unit of migration: bucket 532 is something you can fence, copy, checksum and flip.

201
00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:49.781
The cost: a small map (1,024 entries) to store, version and distribute.

202
00:14:50.061 --> 00:14:53.730
That's a feature: the map is the single source of truth for routing.

203
00:14:54.010 --> 00:15:05.637
Pick B much larger than the maximum shard count you'll ever want (≥ 10 times headroom, and a power of 2 is convenient), because B can't change without rehashing everything.

204
00:15:06.987 --> 00:15:07.627
Tip:

205
00:15:07.907 --> 00:15:11.384
Rule of thumb. hash mod N only if N is fixed forever.

206
00:15:11.664 --> 00:15:17.000
Consistent hashing with vnodes when nodes come and go automatically (caches, Dynamo-style stores).

207
00:15:18.280 --> 00:15:23.677
Fixed buckets + a versioned map for databases you operate and rebalance deliberately.

208
00:15:25.027 --> 00:15:26.499
Choosing the partition key.

209
00:15:26.779 --> 00:15:30.086
The partition key decides which queries are cheap forever.

210
00:15:30.366 --> 00:15:31.326
A checklist:

211
00:15:31.606 --> 00:15:34.209
List the queries by volume and latency need.

212
00:15:34.489 --> 00:15:49.038
For orders: my orders (customer, very high), get order by id (very high), checkout transaction (order + items + payment, high), store's open orders (store, medium), all orders yesterday (analytics, low, offline).

213
00:15:49.318 --> 00:15:58.193
Choose the key that keeps the most important queries and transactions on one partition. customerid keeps my orders, checkout and payments together.

214
00:15:59.543 --> 00:16:01.271
Check the cardinality and skew.

215
00:16:01.551 --> 00:16:06.031
Millions of customers, the biggest one a few hundred orders: good. storeid:

216
00:16:06.311 --> 00:16:17.383
500 stores, one with 5 % of all orders: bad as a primary key (L1 C: the busiest of 64 shards carries 3.90x the average).

217
00:16:17.663 --> 00:16:19.113
Check write distribution.

218
00:16:19.393 --> 00:16:22.657
A timestamp key concentrates all writes (L1 D).

219
00:16:22.937 --> 00:16:33.881
Plan every other query: give it its own partitioned copy (a read model), a global index, a bounded scatter-gather, or send it to the analytics store (Ch 21).

220
00:16:35.231 --> 00:16:36.341
Compound keys.

221
00:16:36.621 --> 00:16:47.287
Cassandra-style PRIMARY KEY ((city, riderid), ts): the partition key (city, riderid) picks the node, and the clustering key ts orders rows inside it.

222
00:16:47.567 --> 00:16:56.357
The same pattern appears in PostgreSQL as PRIMARY KEY (customerid, id) with an index on (customerid, createdat desc).

223
00:16:56.637 --> 00:16:57.703
Co-location.

224
00:16:57.983 --> 00:17:09.525
Tables that are joined or written in one transaction should share the partition key: orders, orderitems and payments all by customerid (Citus calls this co-located tables).

225
00:17:09.805 --> 00:17:16.055
Then checkout is a local transaction, and no distributed transaction (Ch 13) is needed.

226
00:17:17.405 --> 00:17:19.368
Routing: who knows where a key lives?

227
00:17:19.648 --> 00:17:20.992
Here is a quick table:

228
00:17:21.272 --> 00:17:24.067
Approach, and How, and Examples, and Trade-offs.

229
00:17:24.347 --> 00:17:25.627
Client-side routing.

230
00:17:25.907 --> 00:17:26.568
How:

231
00:17:26.848 --> 00:17:30.325
The client library holds the map and connects straight to the shard.

232
00:17:30.605 --> 00:17:31.565
Examples:

233
00:17:31.845 --> 00:17:36.645
Redis Cluster smart clients, Cassandra drivers (token-aware), our ShardRouter.

234
00:17:36.925 --> 00:17:37.885
Trade-offs:

235
00:17:38.165 --> 00:17:39.509
Fastest (no hop).

236
00:17:39.789 --> 00:17:43.672
Every client must refresh the map, and stale maps must be detected.

237
00:17:43.952 --> 00:17:45.445
Proxy or router tier.

238
00:17:45.725 --> 00:17:46.387
How:

239
00:17:46.667 --> 00:17:48.843
Clients talk to a router that knows the map.

240
00:17:49.123 --> 00:17:50.083
Examples:

241
00:17:50.363 --> 00:17:55.120
Vitess vtgate, MongoDB mongos, Citus coordinator, Twemproxy.

242
00:17:55.400 --> 00:17:56.360
Trade-offs:

243
00:17:56.640 --> 00:17:59.008
Simple clients, one place to change.

244
00:17:59.288 --> 00:18:02.445
Extra hop, and the router tier must scale and be HA.

245
00:18:02.725 --> 00:18:03.941
Any node forwards.

246
00:18:04.221 --> 00:18:04.883
How:

247
00:18:05.163 --> 00:18:08.363
Any node accepts the request and forwards or redirects.

248
00:18:08.643 --> 00:18:09.603
Examples:

249
00:18:09.883 --> 00:18:12.549
Cassandra coordinator, Redis Cluster MOVED.

250
00:18:12.829 --> 00:18:13.789
Trade-offs:

251
00:18:14.069 --> 00:18:15.285
No special tier.

252
00:18:15.565 --> 00:18:17.101
An extra hop on a miss.

253
00:18:18.451 --> 00:18:30.697
Where the map lives: a coordination service (ZooKeeper, etcd, Consul, Ch 12), a config table, or the database itself (Redis Cluster gossips the slot map between nodes).

254
00:18:30.977 --> 00:18:33.323
Stale maps are normal, not exceptional.

255
00:18:33.603 --> 00:18:36.057
The design must make them safe and self-healing:

256
00:18:36.337 --> 00:18:39.622
The node that no longer owns the key refuses (Redis:

257
00:18:39.902 --> 00:18:48.115
MOVED 4133 127.0.0.1:7002, our fence:

258
00:18:48.395 --> 00:18:52.086
BUCKETMOVED bucket=40 movedto=shardB).

259
00:18:52.366 --> 00:18:54.905
The client refreshes its map and retries.

260
00:18:55.185 --> 00:18:58.961
The map carries a version, so a client never goes back to an older one.

261
00:19:00.311 --> 00:19:02.999
Routing by id without a global index.

262
00:19:03.279 --> 00:19:06.265
GET or orders/id doesn't know the customer.

263
00:19:06.545 --> 00:19:07.420
Options:

264
00:19:07.700 --> 00:19:12.777
A global orderid, then customerid lookup table (another store to keep consistent).

265
00:19:13.057 --> 00:19:16.108
A scatter-gather on every id lookup (expensive).

266
00:19:17.458 --> 00:19:33.650
Put the bucket in the id: ord2141a0de1f7a40df6a5d04 carries bucket 0x214 = 532, the same bucket as its customer.

267
00:19:33.930 --> 00:19:36.469
Any service can route the id with no lookup.

268
00:19:36.749 --> 00:19:43.661
(Instagram's ids embed a logical shard, and Twitter's Snowflake embeds a worker id for the same reason.

269
00:19:43.941 --> 00:19:46.159
Ch 20 covers id design.).

270
00:19:47.509 --> 00:19:50.347
Secondary indexes: local versus global.

271
00:19:50.627 --> 00:19:55.960
Partitioning by customer makes orders of store-7 a query on a non-partition key.

272
00:19:57.310 --> 00:19:58.654
Here is a quick table:

273
00:19:58.934 --> 00:20:03.563
Local (document-partitioned) index, and Global (term-partitioned) index.

274
00:20:03.843 --> 00:20:04.505
What.

275
00:20:04.785 --> 00:20:07.067
Local (document-partitioned) index:

276
00:20:07.347 --> 00:20:09.801
Each partition indexes only its own rows.

277
00:20:10.081 --> 00:20:11.979
Global (term-partitioned) index:

278
00:20:12.259 --> 00:20:17.059
One index over all data, itself partitioned by the indexed value (storeid).

279
00:20:17.339 --> 00:20:18.086
Write.

280
00:20:18.366 --> 00:20:20.649
Local (document-partitioned) index:

281
00:20:20.929 --> 00:20:22.913
Local, in the same transaction.

282
00:20:23.193 --> 00:20:25.091
Global (term-partitioned) index:

283
00:20:25.371 --> 00:20:30.107
Touches another partition: async (event-fed) or a distributed transaction.

284
00:20:30.387 --> 00:20:31.497
Read by store.

285
00:20:31.777 --> 00:20:34.059
Local (document-partitioned) index:

286
00:20:34.339 --> 00:20:36.878
Scatter-gather over all partitions (lab A:

287
00:20:37.158 --> 00:20:44.177
2.818 ms over 8 partitions versus 0.138 ms for one; lab B:

288
00:20:44.457 --> 00:20:48.083
170 + 230 rows from two shards).

289
00:20:48.363 --> 00:20:50.262
Global (term-partitioned) index:

290
00:20:50.542 --> 00:20:51.587
One partition.

291
00:20:51.867 --> 00:20:52.934
Consistency.

292
00:20:53.214 --> 00:20:55.497
Local (document-partitioned) index:

293
00:20:55.777 --> 00:20:57.014
Same as the data.

294
00:20:57.294 --> 00:20:59.193
Global (term-partitioned) index:

295
00:20:59.473 --> 00:21:04.998
Usually eventually consistent (DynamoDB GSIs, our event-fed read model).

296
00:21:06.348 --> 00:21:07.308
Examples.

297
00:21:07.588 --> 00:21:09.871
Local (document-partitioned) index:

298
00:21:10.151 --> 00:21:16.721
PostgreSQL partitioned indexes, Elasticsearch, MongoDB, Cassandra secondary indexes.

299
00:21:17.001 --> 00:21:18.900
Global (term-partitioned) index:

300
00:21:19.180 --> 00:21:29.164
DynamoDB GSI, our ordersbystore table, Spanner or CockroachDB secondary indexes (transactional, at the cost of distributed writes).

301
00:21:30.514 --> 00:21:51.271
Scatter-gather cost grows with the partition count, and tail latency grows faster: the query is as slow as the slowest partition, so if each shard is slow 1 % of the time, a 64-way fan-out is slow ~47 % of the time (1 minus 0.99⁶⁴), which Dean and Barroso call the tail at scale.

302
00:21:51.551 --> 00:21:59.167
Keep scatter-gathers rare, bounded (a top-K with a limit per shard, a deadline, partial results flagged) and off hot paths.

303
00:22:00.517 --> 00:22:02.288
Hot partitions and hot keys.

304
00:22:02.568 --> 00:22:03.955
Hashing spreads keys.

305
00:22:04.235 --> 00:22:06.091
It can't spread load within one key.

306
00:22:07.441 --> 00:22:08.785
Here is a quick table:

307
00:22:09.065 --> 00:22:11.198
Cause, and Example, and Mitigation.

308
00:22:11.478 --> 00:22:12.417
Skewed key.

309
00:22:12.697 --> 00:22:13.571
Example:

310
00:22:13.851 --> 00:22:16.198
The mega store has 5 % of orders.

311
00:22:16.478 --> 00:22:17.481
Mitigation:

312
00:22:17.761 --> 00:22:22.731
Salt or split the key: store-MEGA0..N, and read all salts (verified:

313
00:22:23.011 --> 00:22:26.979
4.11x, then 1.68x with 4 salts).

314
00:22:27.259 --> 00:22:28.411
Monotonic key.

315
00:22:28.691 --> 00:22:29.566
Example:

316
00:22:29.846 --> 00:22:33.003
Range partitions by time, auto-increment ids.

317
00:22:33.283 --> 00:22:34.286
Mitigation:

318
00:22:34.566 --> 00:22:37.190
Hash, or prefix the key with a hash or bucket.

319
00:22:37.470 --> 00:22:39.347
Celebrity or viral item.

320
00:22:39.627 --> 00:22:40.502
Example:

321
00:22:40.782 --> 00:22:43.875
A flash-sale SKU read 50 K times a second.

322
00:22:44.155 --> 00:22:45.158
Mitigation:

323
00:22:45.438 --> 00:22:50.921
Cache and replicate the key (Ch 07), request coalescing, read replicas.

324
00:22:51.201 --> 00:22:52.225
Hot counter.

325
00:22:52.505 --> 00:22:53.379
Example:

326
00:22:53.659 --> 00:22:55.601
A per-store orders today counter.

327
00:22:55.881 --> 00:22:56.883
Mitigation:

328
00:22:57.163 --> 00:23:01.323
Sharded counters (N sub-counters summed on read), or batch updates.

329
00:23:01.603 --> 00:23:02.841
Unlucky placement.

330
00:23:03.121 --> 00:23:03.995
Example:

331
00:23:04.275 --> 00:23:06.323
Several busy buckets on one shard.

332
00:23:06.603 --> 00:23:07.606
Mitigation:

333
00:23:07.886 --> 00:23:11.385
Move buckets (the bucket map makes this a routine operation).

334
00:23:12.735 --> 00:23:16.980
Salting has a price: reads of that key become a fan-out over its salts.

335
00:23:17.260 --> 00:23:23.340
Salt only the keys that need it, from a config list driven by metrics, and keep the number of salts small.

336
00:23:24.690 --> 00:23:27.335
Rebalancing: moving data while it's being written.

337
00:23:27.615 --> 00:23:30.453
Rebalancing is where sharded systems lose data.

338
00:23:30.733 --> 00:23:40.717
The naive sequence copy, then flip the map, then delete has a window: a writer with the old map writes to the source after the copy and before it learns about the flip.

339
00:23:40.997 --> 00:23:43.834
That write lands in a copy that's about to be deleted.

340
00:23:44.114 --> 00:23:51.410
The simulation in L4 lost 88 of 24,456 acknowledged writes this way.

341
00:23:51.690 --> 00:23:53.311
A safe move of one bucket:

342
00:23:53.591 --> 00:23:55.170
Bulk copy while writable.

343
00:23:55.450 --> 00:23:57.157
Copy the bucket to the target.

344
00:23:57.437 --> 00:24:00.509
Record a watermark (a change sequence or LSN).

345
00:24:01.859 --> 00:24:02.712
Catch up.

346
00:24:02.992 --> 00:24:07.856
Copy rows changed since the watermark, again and again, until the delta is small.

347
00:24:08.136 --> 00:24:13.491
(Production tools use CDC or logical replication for this, see Ch 13.).

348
00:24:13.771 --> 00:24:14.880
Fence the source.

349
00:24:15.160 --> 00:24:22.776
From now on, the source rejects writes to the bucket (a trigger, a flag checked in the write path, or Redis's MIGRATING state).

350
00:24:23.056 --> 00:24:26.149
Writers get BUCKETMOVED, reload the map and retry.

351
00:24:26.429 --> 00:24:29.907
Copy the final delta, then verify (count + checksum).

352
00:24:30.187 --> 00:24:32.811
Flip the map (version + 1) and publish it.

353
00:24:33.091 --> 00:24:35.864
Delete from the source later, after a grace period.

354
00:24:37.214 --> 00:24:40.841
The write freeze is only steps 3 to 5 for one small delta:

355
00:24:41.121 --> 00:24:50.081
36 ms max per bucket in the L4 simulation, versus ~230 ms in the lab's simpler fence-first variant.

356
00:24:50.361 --> 00:24:56.462
Moving one bucket at a time keeps the freeze per customer tiny and the rest of the system unaffected.

357
00:24:57.812 --> 00:25:04.916
Redis Cluster does the same thing per hash slot: the source marks the slot MIGRATING and the target marks it IMPORTING.

358
00:25:05.196 --> 00:25:07.223
Keys move one by one with MIGRATE.

359
00:25:07.503 --> 00:25:18.084
While the slot is in flight, the source answers requests for already moved keys with ASK slot target, which means try the target once, for this request only, with ASKING.

360
00:25:18.364 --> 00:25:21.308
Keys not yet moved are still served by the source.

361
00:25:21.588 --> 00:25:28.735
When the slot is done, SETSLOT NODE makes the target the owner, and the source answers with MOVED, which means update your map.

362
00:25:29.015 --> 00:25:31.340
The lab shows every one of these replies.

363
00:25:32.690 --> 00:25:35.698
Partitioning inside one PostgreSQL server.

364
00:25:35.978 --> 00:25:42.826
Declarative partitioning (PARTITION BY RANGE LIST HASH) splits a table into child tables on one server:

365
00:25:43.106 --> 00:25:50.295
Partition pruning: a query with the partition key scans only matching partitions (lab A: ordersp1 only).

366
00:25:50.575 --> 00:25:52.175
Without the key, all of them.

367
00:25:52.455 --> 00:25:55.997
Maintenance: vacuum, reindex and backup per partition.

368
00:25:56.277 --> 00:25:57.215
Retention:

369
00:25:57.495 --> 00:25:59.010
DETACH or DROP a month.

370
00:26:00.360 --> 00:26:09.661
Uniqueness rule: a unique or primary key must include all partition key columns, because each partition enforces uniqueness only locally.

371
00:26:09.941 --> 00:26:13.269
Lab A shows the error for a primary key on id alone.

372
00:26:13.549 --> 00:26:20.035
Global uniqueness of id alone must come from the id generator (Ch 20), not a constraint.

373
00:26:20.315 --> 00:26:26.203
Too many partitions slow planning and use more memory, so keep it to hundreds, not tens of thousands.

374
00:26:26.483 --> 00:26:27.827
It doesn't add servers.

375
00:26:28.107 --> 00:26:39.755
For that you need Citus (distributed tables over PostgreSQL workers), application-level sharding (this chapter's L3 or L4), or a distributed SQL database.

376
00:26:41.105 --> 00:26:42.577
Choosing a sharding approach.

377
00:26:42.857 --> 00:26:44.201
Here is a quick table:

378
00:26:44.481 --> 00:26:47.489
Option, and What you get, and What you give up, and Fit.

379
00:26:47.769 --> 00:26:50.905
Stay on one primary + partitioning + replicas.

380
00:26:51.185 --> 00:26:52.102
What you get:

381
00:26:52.382 --> 00:26:55.411
Simplicity, full SQL, local transactions.

382
00:26:55.691 --> 00:26:56.651
What you give up:

383
00:26:56.931 --> 00:26:59.043
Write or storage ceiling of one machine.

384
00:26:59.323 --> 00:26:59.963
Fit:

385
00:27:00.243 --> 00:27:02.761
Most systems, for longer than people think.

386
00:27:03.041 --> 00:27:05.835
Application-level sharding (buckets + map).

387
00:27:06.115 --> 00:27:07.033
What you get:

388
00:27:07.313 --> 00:27:10.662
Full control, any SQL engine, predictable moves.

389
00:27:10.942 --> 00:27:11.902
What you give up:

390
00:27:12.182 --> 00:27:15.318
You build routing, moves, cross-shard queries, tooling.

391
00:27:15.598 --> 00:27:16.238
Fit:

392
00:27:16.518 --> 00:27:19.569
Clear partition key, a team to own it (this chapter).

393
00:27:19.849 --> 00:27:22.985
Middleware sharding (Vitess, Citus, ShardingSphere).

394
00:27:23.265 --> 00:27:24.182
What you get:

395
00:27:24.462 --> 00:27:27.811
Routing, resharding and some cross-shard queries built in.

396
00:27:28.091 --> 00:27:29.051
What you give up:

397
00:27:29.331 --> 00:27:32.339
Another system to operate; SQL limitations.

398
00:27:32.619 --> 00:27:33.259
Fit:

399
00:27:33.539 --> 00:27:38.659
MySQL (Vitess), PostgreSQL (Citus), JDBC (ShardingSphere).

400
00:27:38.939 --> 00:27:43.889
Distributed SQL (Spanner, CockroachDB, YugabyteDB, TiDB).

401
00:27:44.169 --> 00:27:45.086
What you get:

402
00:27:45.366 --> 00:27:50.315
Automatic range splits and moves, distributed transactions, global indexes.

403
00:27:50.595 --> 00:27:51.555
What you give up:

404
00:27:51.835 --> 00:27:55.633
Latency on cross-range transactions; cost; new ops skills.

405
00:27:55.913 --> 00:27:56.553
Fit:

406
00:27:56.833 --> 00:28:01.014
Multi-region, many access patterns, strong consistency needs.

407
00:28:02.364 --> 00:28:07.655
NoSQL with native partitioning (DynamoDB, Cassandra, MongoDB).

408
00:28:07.935 --> 00:28:08.852
What you get:

409
00:28:09.132 --> 00:28:11.073
Partitioning is the data model.

410
00:28:11.353 --> 00:28:12.313
What you give up:

411
00:28:12.593 --> 00:28:15.111
Query flexibility; you still choose the key.

412
00:28:15.391 --> 00:28:16.031
Fit:

413
00:28:16.311 --> 00:28:20.364
Key-value or wide-column access patterns (Ch 06).

414
00:28:21.714 --> 00:28:22.610
Part 3.

415
00:28:22.890 --> 00:28:24.618
Architecture and Diagrams.

416
00:28:25.868 --> 00:28:27.873
Component view: sharded orders.

417
00:28:28.153 --> 00:28:29.497
Here is the diagram.

418
00:28:33.347 --> 00:28:36.910
Every shard is itself a replicated cluster (Ch 10).

419
00:28:37.190 --> 00:28:42.993
The shard map is small (1,024 rows plus a version) and cached in every pod.

420
00:28:43.273 --> 00:28:50.270
Store queries go to a separately partitioned read model fed by events, not to a scatter-gather over the order shards.

421
00:28:50.550 --> 00:28:54.838
Scatter-gather is kept for rare support and admin queries, with a deadline.

422
00:28:56.188 --> 00:28:59.751
Sequence: happy path, place an order and read it back by id.

423
00:29:00.031 --> 00:29:02.825
The sequence diagram walks through it step by step.

