01:06:27.103 --> 01:06:29.172
Harness + Lab B + Lab C.

914
01:06:29.452 --> 01:06:30.327
L4:

915
01:06:30.607 --> 01:06:33.764
Harness with concurrent writers + Labs B and C.

916
01:06:35.114 --> 01:06:36.053
Part 6.

917
01:06:36.333 --> 01:06:37.613
Production Concerns.

918
01:06:38.863 --> 01:06:40.015
Failure modes.

919
01:06:40.295 --> 01:06:41.639
Here is a quick table:

920
01:06:41.919 --> 01:06:45.801
Failure, and Detection signal, and Blast radius, and Mitigation.

921
01:06:46.081 --> 01:06:48.364
Writes lost during a move (no fence).

922
01:06:48.644 --> 01:06:49.839
Detection signal:

923
01:06:50.119 --> 01:06:54.791
Reconciliation finds rows only on the old shard; missing order tickets.

924
01:06:55.071 --> 01:06:56.287
Blast radius:

925
01:06:56.567 --> 01:06:58.785
Silent data loss (L4 naive:

926
01:06:59.065 --> 01:07:00.260
88 writes).

927
01:07:00.540 --> 01:07:01.543
Mitigation:

928
01:07:01.823 --> 01:07:07.177
Fence + final delta + checksum before flip; delete only after a grace period.

929
01:07:07.457 --> 01:07:09.143
Stale shard map in a pod.

930
01:07:09.423 --> 01:07:10.617
Detection signal:

931
01:07:10.897 --> 01:07:14.503
BUCKETMOVED or MOVED rate; map version skew across pods.

932
01:07:14.783 --> 01:07:15.999
Blast radius:

933
01:07:16.279 --> 01:07:18.988
Retries; errors if the client doesn't refresh.

934
01:07:19.268 --> 01:07:20.271
Mitigation:

935
01:07:20.551 --> 01:07:24.476
Refresh on refusal, poll with a version, versions only increase.

936
01:07:24.756 --> 01:07:26.228
Checksum mismatch at flip.

937
01:07:26.508 --> 01:07:27.703
Detection signal:

938
01:07:27.983 --> 01:07:28.985
Mover alert.

939
01:07:29.265 --> 01:07:30.481
Blast radius:

940
01:07:30.761 --> 01:07:32.873
One bucket's writes blocked while fenced.

941
01:07:33.153 --> 01:07:34.156
Mitigation:

942
01:07:34.436 --> 01:07:37.700
Keep fence, don't flip, page; runbook to diff and repair.

943
01:07:37.980 --> 01:07:38.940
Hot shard.

944
01:07:39.220 --> 01:07:40.415
Detection signal:

945
01:07:40.695 --> 01:07:44.961
Per-shard CPU or latency skew; top-N buckets by QPS.

946
01:07:45.241 --> 01:07:46.457
Blast radius:

947
01:07:46.737 --> 01:07:49.980
Everyone on that shard (~1 or N of customers).

948
01:07:50.260 --> 01:07:51.263
Mitigation:

949
01:07:51.543 --> 01:07:54.081
Move hot buckets; salt hot keys; cache.

950
01:07:55.431 --> 01:07:56.562
One shard down.

951
01:07:56.842 --> 01:07:58.037
Detection signal:

952
01:07:58.317 --> 01:08:00.941
Health checks; errors on 1 or N of buckets.

953
01:08:01.221 --> 01:08:04.591
Blast radius: ~1 or N of customers, not all.

954
01:08:04.871 --> 01:08:05.874
Mitigation:

955
01:08:06.154 --> 01:08:15.626
Each shard is a replicated cluster (Ch 10); degrade per shard; don't let one shard's timeouts exhaust shared pools (bulkheads, Ch 18).

956
01:08:15.906 --> 01:08:17.613
Scatter-gather tail latency.

957
01:08:17.893 --> 01:08:19.087
Detection signal:

958
01:08:19.367 --> 01:08:22.695
Fan-out p99 ≫ single-shard p99.

959
01:08:22.975 --> 01:08:24.191
Blast radius:

960
01:08:24.471 --> 01:08:28.589
Admin or support endpoints; pool exhaustion if on a hot path.

961
01:08:28.869 --> 01:08:29.871
Mitigation:

962
01:08:30.151 --> 01:08:33.970
Deadlines, per-shard limits, partial results, move to a read model.

963
01:08:34.250 --> 01:08:35.658
Cross-shard write needed.

964
01:08:35.938 --> 01:08:37.133
Detection signal:

965
01:08:37.413 --> 01:08:40.143
Code review; transaction spans two shards.

966
01:08:40.423 --> 01:08:41.639
Blast radius:

967
01:08:41.919 --> 01:08:44.053
Inconsistency on partial failure.

968
01:08:44.333 --> 01:08:45.335
Mitigation:

969
01:08:45.615 --> 01:08:49.967
Co-locate by key; saga or outbox for the rest (Ch 13).

970
01:08:51.317 --> 01:08:53.664
Bucket function changed by accident.

971
01:08:53.944 --> 01:08:55.139
Detection signal:

972
01:08:55.419 --> 01:08:58.619
Golden-value test fails; routing to wrong shards.

973
01:08:58.899 --> 01:09:00.115
Blast radius:

974
01:09:00.395 --> 01:09:03.083
Every key misrouted: data missing everywhere.

975
01:09:03.363 --> 01:09:04.365
Mitigation:

976
01:09:04.645 --> 01:09:08.741
Pin with golden tests; function lives in one versioned library.

977
01:09:09.021 --> 01:09:11.219
Duplicate ids across partitions.

978
01:09:11.499 --> 01:09:12.693
Detection signal:

979
01:09:12.973 --> 01:09:16.195
Constraint can't catch it (PK includes partition key).

980
01:09:16.475 --> 01:09:17.691
Blast radius:

981
01:09:17.971 --> 01:09:19.827
Wrong order shown or updated.

982
01:09:20.107 --> 01:09:21.109
Mitigation:

983
01:09:21.389 --> 01:09:25.741
Globally unique id generator (Ch 20); id carries bucket.

984
01:09:26.021 --> 01:09:27.728
Redis CROSSSLOT in production.

985
01:09:28.008 --> 01:09:29.203
Detection signal:

986
01:09:29.483 --> 01:09:31.296
Errors after moving to Cluster.

987
01:09:31.576 --> 01:09:32.792
Blast radius:

988
01:09:33.072 --> 01:09:34.544
Multi-key ops fail.

989
01:09:34.824 --> 01:09:35.827
Mitigation:

990
01:09:36.107 --> 01:09:40.437
Hash tags for keys used together; no multi-key ops across entities.

991
01:09:40.717 --> 01:09:41.912
Read model lag.

992
01:09:42.192 --> 01:09:43.387
Detection signal:

993
01:09:43.667 --> 01:09:44.691
Consumer lag.

994
01:09:44.971 --> 01:09:46.187
Blast radius:

995
01:09:46.467 --> 01:09:47.896
Stale store dashboard.

996
01:09:48.176 --> 01:09:49.179
Mitigation:

997
01:09:49.459 --> 01:09:52.360
Show as of; alert on lag; replay from Kafka.

998
01:09:53.710 --> 01:09:54.798
Observability.

999
01:09:55.078 --> 01:09:55.995
Per shard:

1000
01:09:56.275 --> 01:10:05.278
QPS, p50 or p99 latency, CPU, disk, connections, replication lag (each shard is a Ch 10 cluster).

1001
01:10:05.558 --> 01:10:11.702
Skew: max or avg across shards for QPS and storage, alert when 1.5x.

1002
01:10:11.982 --> 01:10:18.318
Top-N buckets and keys by QPS (sampled), which is how hot keys are found before they're incidents.

1003
01:10:18.598 --> 01:10:25.766
Routing: shard map version per pod (a gauge), BUCKETMOVED or MOVED or ASK rates, retries after refusal.

1004
01:10:27.116 --> 01:10:37.292
Moves: buckets in flight, bytes copied, catch-up rounds, final delta size, freeze time per bucket (histogram), checksum results, grace-period deletes pending.

1005
01:10:37.572 --> 01:10:41.860
Fan-out: shards touched per request (a histogram, tagged by endpoint).

1006
01:10:42.140 --> 01:10:47.388
A new endpoint that fans out to every shard shows up here before it shows up in latency.

1007
01:10:47.668 --> 01:10:51.017
Read models: consumer lag in seconds per partition.

1008
01:10:52.367 --> 01:10:53.306
Security.

1009
01:10:53.586 --> 01:11:05.639
Shards are a tenant isolation tool too: a big B2B customer or a regulated region can get dedicated buckets on a dedicated shard (data residency, Ch 36).

1010
01:11:05.919 --> 01:11:07.925
Each shard has its own credentials.

1011
01:11:08.205 --> 01:11:13.346
The mover's account needs read on source, write on target and bucketfence, nothing more.

1012
01:11:13.626 --> 01:11:16.037
Application pods never get DDL.

1013
01:11:16.317 --> 01:11:19.346
Order ids that carry a bucket leak a little structure.

1014
01:11:19.626 --> 01:11:28.437
Keep a random suffix (as OrderIds does) so ids can't be guessed, and always check ownership (customerid from the token) after routing.

1015
01:11:29.787 --> 01:11:35.909
Scatter-gather admin endpoints are a data-exfiltration path: authorize, rate-limit, and audit them.

1016
01:11:36.189 --> 01:11:42.568
Backups and PITR are per shard: a restore must be coordinated with the shard map version at that time.

1017
01:11:43.918 --> 01:11:45.582
Performance and tuning knobs.

1018
01:11:45.862 --> 01:11:47.206
Here is a quick table:

1019
01:11:47.486 --> 01:11:49.470
Knob, and Default here, and Effect.

1020
01:11:49.750 --> 01:11:50.966
Number of buckets.

1021
01:11:51.246 --> 01:11:52.163
Default here:

1022
01:11:52.443 --> 01:11:53.937
1,024.

1023
01:11:54.217 --> 01:12:00.339
Effect: ≥ 10 times max shards; more buckets = finer moves, bigger map.

1024
01:12:00.619 --> 01:12:02.134
Buckets moved in parallel.

1025
01:12:02.414 --> 01:12:03.331
Default here:

1026
01:12:03.611 --> 01:12:04.891
1 per shard pair.

1027
01:12:05.171 --> 01:12:05.961
Effect:

1028
01:12:06.241 --> 01:12:09.889
More = faster rebalance, more I or O on live shards.

1029
01:12:10.169 --> 01:12:11.427
Catch-up threshold.

1030
01:12:11.707 --> 01:12:12.625
Default here:

1031
01:12:12.905 --> 01:12:16.147
20 rows (sim) / ~1 MB (prod).

1032
01:12:16.427 --> 01:12:17.217
Effect:

1033
01:12:17.497 --> 01:12:20.035
Smaller = shorter freeze, more rounds.

1034
01:12:20.315 --> 01:12:21.894
Grace period before delete.

1035
01:12:22.174 --> 01:12:23.091
Default here:

1036
01:12:23.371 --> 01:12:24.523
24 h.

1037
01:12:24.803 --> 01:12:25.593
Effect:

1038
01:12:25.873 --> 01:12:28.305
Longer = safer rollback, more disk.

1039
01:12:28.585 --> 01:12:29.801
Map poll interval.

1040
01:12:30.081 --> 01:12:30.998
Default here:

1041
01:12:31.278 --> 01:12:32.217
5 s.

1042
01:12:32.497 --> 01:12:33.286
Effect:

1043
01:12:33.566 --> 01:12:36.702
Shorter = fewer refusals, more config reads.

1044
01:12:38.052 --> 01:12:39.396
Scatter-gather deadline.

1045
01:12:39.676 --> 01:12:40.593
Default here:

1046
01:12:40.873 --> 01:12:42.196
300 ms.

1047
01:12:42.476 --> 01:12:43.265
Effect:

1048
01:12:43.545 --> 01:12:45.593
Shorter = more partial results.

1049
01:12:45.873 --> 01:12:48.241
PostgreSQL partitions per table.

1050
01:12:48.521 --> 01:12:49.439
Default here:

1051
01:12:49.719 --> 01:12:51.041
8 to 64.

1052
01:12:51.321 --> 01:12:52.111
Effect:

1053
01:12:52.391 --> 01:12:56.273
Too many = slower planning; too few = big partitions.

1054
01:12:56.553 --> 01:12:57.663
Hot-key salts.

1055
01:12:57.943 --> 01:12:58.860
Default here:

1056
01:12:59.140 --> 01:13:00.825
4 to 8 for listed keys.

1057
01:13:01.105 --> 01:13:01.895
Effect:

1058
01:13:02.175 --> 01:13:04.543
More = flatter load, wider reads.

1059
01:13:04.823 --> 01:13:05.932
Connection pools.

1060
01:13:06.212 --> 01:13:07.129
Default here:

1061
01:13:07.409 --> 01:13:09.543
One per shard, sized per shard.

1062
01:13:09.823 --> 01:13:10.612
Effect:

1063
01:13:10.892 --> 01:13:18.871
Total connections = pods times shards times pool size: watch the database's maxconnections, use PgBouncer.

1064
01:13:20.221 --> 01:13:20.946
Cost.

1065
01:13:21.226 --> 01:13:22.975
Shards multiply everything:

1066
01:13:23.255 --> 01:13:31.469
4 shards times (primary + 2 standbys) = 12 database servers, plus monitoring, backups and on-call surface.

1067
01:13:31.749 --> 01:13:35.802
Sharding too early is one of the most expensive mistakes in this handbook.

1068
01:13:36.082 --> 01:13:40.050
Connections: pods times shards times pool size grows fast.

1069
01:13:40.330 --> 01:13:45.023
A pooler (PgBouncer) per shard is usually needed past a few shards.

1070
01:13:45.303 --> 01:13:53.218
Read models double storage for the data they copy and add a consumer, but they are much cheaper than making every store query a fan-out.

1071
01:13:53.498 --> 01:13:56.271
Rebalancing costs I or O on live shards.

1072
01:13:56.551 --> 01:13:59.069
Schedule moves off-peak and rate-limit them.

1073
01:14:00.419 --> 01:14:11.149
Engineering: the router, mover, tooling, cross-shard reporting, per-shard migrations (schema changes must run on every shard, and be backwards-compatible while they roll).

1074
01:14:12.499 --> 01:14:13.438
Part 7.

1075
01:14:13.718 --> 01:14:15.425
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

1076
01:14:16.675 --> 01:14:19.000
1. hash(key) % numberofshards.

1077
01:14:19.280 --> 01:14:20.091
Symptom:

1078
01:14:20.371 --> 01:14:27.560
Adding a shard moves ~80 % of the data (L1), for days, with lost writes (§1.1).

1079
01:14:28.910 --> 01:14:38.339
Here is the Java code. before: the shard count is part of the key's address. after: the address is a fixed bucket; only the small map changes.

1080
01:14:41.189 --> 01:14:41.893
2.

1081
01:14:42.173 --> 01:14:43.923
Moving data without a fence.

1082
01:14:44.203 --> 01:14:45.013
Symptom:

1083
01:14:45.293 --> 01:14:50.200
Writes that arrive between the copy and the map flip go to the old copy and vanish.

1084
01:14:50.480 --> 01:14:52.741
No errors are raised (L4 naive:

1085
01:14:53.021 --> 01:14:54.195
88 lost).

1086
01:14:54.475 --> 01:14:55.200
Fix:

1087
01:14:55.480 --> 01:15:00.643
Copy, catch up, fence the source, copy the final delta, verify, flip, delete later.

1088
01:15:00.923 --> 01:15:01.669
3.

1089
01:15:01.949 --> 01:15:04.531
Sharding by a low-cardinality or skewed key.

1090
01:15:04.811 --> 01:15:05.621
Symptom:

1091
01:15:05.901 --> 01:15:10.488
A few shards run hot forever, and adding shards makes it worse (L1 C:

1092
01:15:10.768 --> 01:15:14.267
1.11x, then 3.90x).

1093
01:15:14.547 --> 01:15:15.272
Fix:

1094
01:15:15.552 --> 01:15:17.771
Pick a high-cardinality key (customer).

1095
01:15:18.051 --> 01:15:25.880
Give store-level queries their own read model, and salt the hot keys (4.11x, then 1.68x).

1096
01:15:26.160 --> 01:15:26.864
4.

1097
01:15:27.144 --> 01:15:28.765
Using an unstable hash.

1098
01:15:30.115 --> 01:15:32.121
Here is the Java code. before:

1099
01:15:32.401 --> 01:15:43.238
Object.hashCode() of a record or entity, or a language whose string hash is randomized per process. after: a named hash over bytes, pinned by a golden test.

1100
01:15:46.088 --> 01:15:46.856
5.

1101
01:15:47.136 --> 01:15:51.936
A primary key that doesn't include the partition key, fixed by dropping the constraint.

1102
01:15:52.216 --> 01:15:53.027
Symptom:

1103
01:15:53.307 --> 01:15:57.360
PostgreSQL refuses a PK on id alone (lab A).

1104
01:15:57.640 --> 01:16:02.163
Someone drops the constraint, and duplicate ids appear on different partitions.

1105
01:16:02.443 --> 01:16:03.168
Fix:

1106
01:16:03.448 --> 01:16:09.208
PRIMARY KEY (customerid, id) and a globally unique id generator (Ch 20).

1107
01:16:09.488 --> 01:16:10.235
6.

1108
01:16:10.515 --> 01:16:12.115
Scatter-gather on a hot path.

1109
01:16:12.395 --> 01:16:13.205
Symptom:

1110
01:16:13.485 --> 01:16:23.021
The store dashboard fans out to every shard every 10 seconds per open dashboard. p99 tracks the slowest shard, and load grows with the shard count.

1111
01:16:23.301 --> 01:16:24.027
Fix:

1112
01:16:24.307 --> 01:16:27.528
An event-fed read model partitioned by the query's key.

1113
01:16:27.808 --> 01:16:31.968
Keep scatter-gather for rare, bounded, deadline-limited admin queries.

1114
01:16:33.318 --> 01:16:34.065
7.

1115
01:16:34.345 --> 01:16:36.670
Cross-shard transactions by accident.

1116
01:16:36.950 --> 01:16:37.761
Symptom:

1117
01:16:38.041 --> 01:16:44.377
Transfer wallet credit from customer A to B opens two connections and commits both, and one commit fails.

1118
01:16:44.657 --> 01:16:45.382
Fix:

1119
01:16:45.662 --> 01:16:47.838
Co-locate data that's written together.

1120
01:16:48.118 --> 01:16:54.347
For truly cross-key operations, use a saga with an outbox (Ch 13), not two commits.

1121
01:16:54.627 --> 01:16:55.310
8.

1122
01:16:55.590 --> 01:16:57.083
Sharding before it's needed.

1123
01:16:57.363 --> 01:16:58.174
Symptom:

1124
01:16:58.454 --> 01:17:05.302
12 database servers, a routing library and a mover for 200 GB that one primary could handle.

1125
01:17:05.582 --> 01:17:06.307
Fix:

1126
01:17:06.587 --> 01:17:14.737
Ch 09's order: indexes and queries, caching, read replicas, partitioning inside one server, a bigger machine.

1127
01:17:15.017 --> 01:17:20.158
Shard when writes or storage truly exceed one primary, with numbers (§4).

1128
01:17:21.508 --> 01:17:22.255
9.

1129
01:17:22.535 --> 01:17:25.863
Range-partitioning across servers by time or sequence.

1130
01:17:26.143 --> 01:17:26.953
Symptom:

1131
01:17:27.233 --> 01:17:32.311
Every insert goes to the newest shard (L1 D), so one shard does all the writes.

1132
01:17:32.591 --> 01:17:33.316
Fix:

1133
01:17:33.596 --> 01:17:35.559
Hash (or bucket) across servers.

1134
01:17:35.839 --> 01:17:39.593
Range-partition by time inside each shard for retention.

1135
01:17:39.873 --> 01:17:40.577
10.

1136
01:17:40.857 --> 01:17:43.332
Redis multi-key commands without hash tags.

1137
01:17:44.682 --> 01:17:45.599
On screen:

1138
01:17:45.879 --> 01:17:53.282
MGET cust:42:name cust:77:name, then CROSSSLOT Keys in request don't hash to the same. slot.

1139
01:17:53.562 --> 01:18:01.221
MGET cust-42:orders cust-42:cart, then 3 2 (same slot 3330, lab C).

1140
01:18:04.071 --> 01:18:09.105
Don't overuse hash tags either: global: puts every key in one slot on one node.

1141
01:18:10.455 --> 01:18:11.330
Part 8.

1142
01:18:11.610 --> 01:18:12.954
Interview Q and A.

1143
01:18:14.204 --> 01:18:15.036
Beginner.

1144
01:18:15.316 --> 01:18:16.340
Question 1.

1145
01:18:16.620 --> 01:18:20.204
What's the difference between partitioning, sharding and replication?

1146
01:18:20.484 --> 01:18:21.849
Thirty-second answer:

1147
01:18:22.129 --> 01:18:25.372
Partitioning splits a dataset into disjoint pieces.

1148
01:18:25.652 --> 01:18:31.135
Sharding is partitioning across servers, so each server holds and writes only part of the data.

1149
01:18:31.415 --> 01:18:34.913
Replication copies the same data to several servers.

1150
01:18:35.193 --> 01:18:41.145
Replication scales reads and survives failures, and only partitioning scales writes and storage.

1151
01:18:41.425 --> 01:18:44.753
Real systems do both: each shard is replicated.

1152
01:18:46.103 --> 01:18:47.106
Question 2.

1153
01:18:47.386 --> 01:18:49.263
Range versus hash partitioning?

1154
01:18:49.543 --> 01:18:50.909
Thirty-second answer:

1155
01:18:51.189 --> 01:19:02.837
Range partitioning keeps contiguous key ranges together, so range scans are cheap and old ranges drop easily, but monotonic keys (time) send all writes to one partition.

1156
01:19:03.117 --> 01:19:11.885
Hash partitioning spreads keys evenly, including monotonic ones, but any range query or query without the key touches every partition.

1157
01:19:13.235 --> 01:19:14.280
Question 3.

1158
01:19:14.560 --> 01:19:18.827
Why does a query without the partition key get slower as you add partitions?

1159
01:19:19.107 --> 01:19:20.472
Thirty-second answer:

1160
01:19:20.752 --> 01:19:26.939
The router can't tell which partition holds matching rows, so it must ask all of them and merge (scatter-gather).

1161
01:19:27.219 --> 01:19:32.637
The work grows with the partition count, and the response is as slow as the slowest partition.

1162
01:19:33.987 --> 01:19:35.054
Question 4.

1163
01:19:35.334 --> 01:19:38.491
What's a hot partition, and how can it happen with a good hash?

1164
01:19:38.771 --> 01:19:40.137
Thirty-second answer:

1165
01:19:40.417 --> 01:19:43.105
A partition that gets far more load than the others.

1166
01:19:43.385 --> 01:19:53.454
A hash spreads keys evenly, but if one key (a mega store, a viral product) gets a large share of traffic, its partition is hot no matter how good the hash is.

1167
01:19:54.804 --> 01:19:55.807
Intermediate.

1168
01:19:56.087 --> 01:19:57.196
Question 5.

1169
01:19:57.476 --> 01:19:59.097
How do you choose a shard key?

1170
01:19:59.377 --> 01:20:00.743
Thirty-second answer:

1171
01:20:01.023 --> 01:20:02.367
From access patterns.

1172
01:20:02.647 --> 01:20:13.612
List queries and transactions by volume and latency need, and choose the key that keeps the most important ones on one shard, with high cardinality, low skew and spread-out writes.

1173
01:20:13.892 --> 01:20:17.071
Then plan explicitly for every query that doesn't use it.

1174
01:20:18.421 --> 01:20:19.530
Question 6.

1175
01:20:19.810 --> 01:20:22.349
Why is hash mod N a bad sharding function?

1176
01:20:22.629 --> 01:20:23.994
Thirty-second answer:

1177
01:20:24.274 --> 01:20:31.826
Changing N remaps almost every key: a key stays only if h mod N == h mod (N+1).

1178
01:20:32.106 --> 01:20:36.821
L1 measured 80.1 % of keys moving from 4 to 5 shards.

1179
01:20:37.101 --> 01:20:41.047
Consistent hashing or fixed buckets move only ~1 or N.

1180
01:20:42.397 --> 01:20:43.485
Question 7.

1181
01:20:43.765 --> 01:20:47.157
Explain consistent hashing and why virtual nodes matter.

1182
01:20:47.437 --> 01:20:48.803
Thirty-second answer:

1183
01:20:49.083 --> 01:20:54.011
Keys and nodes are hashed onto a ring, and a key belongs to the next node clockwise.

1184
01:20:54.291 --> 01:21:00.029
Adding or removing a node moves only the keys between it and its neighbour, ~1 or N.

1185
01:21:00.309 --> 01:21:05.451
With one point per node, arcs are uneven and a new node takes load from just one neighbour.

1186
01:21:05.731 --> 01:21:11.277
Virtual nodes (many points per node) even out the load and spread the moves across all nodes.

1187
01:21:12.627 --> 01:21:13.651
Question 8.

1188
01:21:13.931 --> 01:21:18.326
Why must a unique constraint on a partitioned table include the partition key?

1189
01:21:18.606 --> 01:21:19.971
Thirty-second answer:

1190
01:21:20.251 --> 01:21:24.113
Each partition enforces uniqueness with its own local index.

1191
01:21:24.393 --> 01:21:32.755
Without the partition key in the constraint, two rows with the same id could sit in different partitions, and no single index would see both.

1192
01:21:33.035 --> 01:21:36.961
PostgreSQL refuses to create it (lab A shows the error).

1193
01:21:38.311 --> 01:21:39.377
Question 9.

1194
01:21:39.657 --> 01:21:42.345
Local versus global secondary indexes?

1195
01:21:42.625 --> 01:21:43.991
Thirty-second answer:

1196
01:21:44.271 --> 01:21:52.228
A local index covers one partition's rows, so writes stay local but reads by the indexed column scatter to all partitions.

1197
01:21:52.508 --> 01:22:05.393
A global index is partitioned by the indexed value, so reads hit one partition but writes touch another partition, usually asynchronously (eventual consistency) or with a distributed transaction.

1198
01:22:06.743 --> 01:22:07.511
Senior.

1199
01:22:07.791 --> 01:22:08.837
Question 10.

1200
01:22:09.117 --> 01:22:14.173
Design sharding for an orders database that must grow from 4 to 16 shards.

1201
01:22:14.453 --> 01:22:15.818
Thirty-second answer:

1202
01:22:16.098 --> 01:22:32.567
Hash customerid into 1,024 fixed buckets, keep a versioned bucket, then shard map, co-locate orders or items or payments by bucket, put the bucket in order ids, and build an online mover (copy, catch up, fence, verify, flip, delete later).

1203
01:22:32.847 --> 01:22:35.386
Store queries use an event-fed read model.

1204
01:22:35.666 --> 01:22:37.949
Each shard is a replicated cluster.

1205
01:22:39.299 --> 01:22:40.429
Question 11.

1206
01:22:40.709 --> 01:22:43.568
How do you route a request that only has an order id?

1207
01:22:43.848 --> 01:22:45.213
Thirty-second answer:

1208
01:22:45.493 --> 01:22:55.371
Encode the bucket in the id at creation (ord214... then bucket 532), so any service can compute the shard without a lookup.

1209
01:22:55.651 --> 01:23:04.845
Alternatives are a global id, then key lookup table (another store to keep consistent and highly available) or a scatter-gather (expensive).

1210
01:23:06.195 --> 01:23:07.283
Question 12.

1211
01:23:07.563 --> 01:23:10.315
How do you run a query across all shards safely?

1212
01:23:10.595 --> 01:23:11.961
Thirty-second answer:

1213
01:23:12.241 --> 01:23:23.654
Fan out in parallel with a deadline, push down filters and a per-shard limit (top-K), merge with a heap, and return partial results flagged as incomplete if a shard is slow or down.

1214
01:23:23.934 --> 01:23:27.774
Keep it off hot paths, rate-limit it, and track fan-out per endpoint.

1215
01:23:29.124 --> 01:23:30.404
Question 13.

1216
01:23:30.684 --> 01:23:34.673
How do you handle a hot key like a mega store or a flash-sale product?

1217
01:23:34.953 --> 01:23:36.319
Thirty-second answer:

1218
01:23:36.599 --> 01:23:38.839
First identify it (per-key metrics).

1219
01:23:39.119 --> 01:23:43.684
For write-heavy keys, split it into N salted sub-keys and read all salts.

1220
01:23:43.964 --> 01:23:46.524
For read-heavy keys, cache and replicate it.

1221
01:23:46.804 --> 01:23:49.599
For a hot bucket, move it to a dedicated shard.

1222
01:23:49.879 --> 01:23:51.969
For counters, use sharded counters.

1223
01:23:53.319 --> 01:23:54.514
Question 14.

1224
01:23:54.794 --> 01:23:58.549
How does Redis Cluster route keys, and what are MOVED and ASK?

1225
01:23:58.829 --> 01:24:00.194
Thirty-second answer:

1226
01:24:00.474 --> 01:24:11.695
Keys map to one of 16,384 slots via CRC16 mod 16384, and each master owns ranges of slots.

1227
01:24:11.975 --> 01:24:17.309
A node that doesn't own a slot replies MOVED slot node, and the client updates its slot map.

1228
01:24:17.589 --> 01:24:22.581
During migration, the source replies ASK slot node for keys already moved.

1229
01:24:22.861 --> 01:24:27.959
The client sends ASKING plus the command to the target, once, without updating its map.

1230
01:24:29.309 --> 01:24:30.205
Architect.

1231
01:24:30.485 --> 01:24:31.680
Question 15.

1232
01:24:31.960 --> 01:24:35.224
How do you move a bucket to a new shard without losing writes?

1233
01:24:35.504 --> 01:24:36.869
Thirty-second answer:

1234
01:24:37.149 --> 01:24:38.728
Bulk copy while writable.

1235
01:24:39.008 --> 01:24:41.077
Catch up deltas until they're small.

1236
01:24:41.357 --> 01:24:44.493
Fence the source for the bucket so writes there are refused.

1237
01:24:44.773 --> 01:24:52.688
Copy the final delta, verify with count + checksum, flip the versioned map, and delete from the source after a grace period.

1238
01:24:52.968 --> 01:24:56.381
Stale writers get BUCKETMOVED, reload the map and retry.

1239
01:24:57.731 --> 01:24:58.926
Question 16.

1240
01:24:59.206 --> 01:25:02.833
How do sharding and replication interact during failures?

1241
01:25:03.113 --> 01:25:04.478
Thirty-second answer:

1242
01:25:04.758 --> 01:25:16.513
Each shard is its own replicated cluster, so a primary failure affects only that shard's buckets (~1 or N), with the Ch 10 guarantees (quorum sync, fenced failover).

1243
01:25:16.793 --> 01:25:23.129
The shard map points at the shard's writer endpoint, not at node IPs, so failover doesn't change the map.

1244
01:25:24.479 --> 01:25:25.823
Question 17.

1245
01:25:26.103 --> 01:25:31.948
When would you choose Vitess, Citus or a distributed SQL database over app-level sharding?

1246
01:25:32.228 --> 01:25:33.593
Thirty-second answer:

1247
01:25:33.873 --> 01:25:36.519
When you'd rather operate a system than build one.

1248
01:25:36.799 --> 01:25:44.628
Vitess (MySQL) and Citus (PostgreSQL) give routing, resharding and some cross-shard queries on familiar engines.

1249
01:25:44.908 --> 01:25:56.065
Distributed SQL (Spanner, CockroachDB, YugabyteDB, TiDB) gives automatic range splitting, rebalancing, distributed transactions and global indexes.

1250
01:25:56.345 --> 01:26:00.804
App-level sharding fits when there's one clear key and the team wants full control.

1251
01:26:02.154 --> 01:26:03.327
Question 18.

1252
01:26:03.607 --> 01:26:06.402
How do you run schema changes on 16 shards?

1253
01:26:06.682 --> 01:26:08.047
Thirty-second answer:

1254
01:26:08.327 --> 01:26:18.759
Every change is expand or contract and backwards-compatible, rolled to one canary shard first, then the rest, with a migration tool that tracks the version per shard.

1255
01:26:19.039 --> 01:26:24.330
The application must work with any mix of old and new schema while the rollout is in progress.

1256
01:26:25.680 --> 01:26:26.917
Question 19.

1257
01:26:27.197 --> 01:26:31.272
Your busiest shard is at 90 % CPU during the evening peak.

1258
01:26:31.552 --> 01:26:32.619
Walk me through it.

1259
01:26:32.899 --> 01:26:34.264
Thirty-second answer:

1260
01:26:34.544 --> 01:26:36.720
Confirm it's one shard (skew metric).

1261
01:26:37.000 --> 01:26:44.083
Find whether it's a few hot buckets or keys (top-N) or an expensive query pattern (fan-out, missing index).

1262
01:26:44.363 --> 01:26:49.056
Short term: cache or rate-limit the offender, move hot buckets to cooler shards.

1263
01:26:49.336 --> 01:26:54.072
Long term: salt the hot key, move the query to a read model, or add a shard.

1264
01:26:55.422 --> 01:26:56.531
Question 20.

1265
01:26:56.811 --> 01:27:00.523
What are the long-term costs of sharding, and how do you keep them in check?

1266
01:27:00.803 --> 01:27:02.169
Thirty-second answer:

1267
01:27:02.449 --> 01:27:15.035
More infrastructure, more on-call, cross-shard queries, per-shard migrations and backups, a routing library in every service, rebalancing tooling, and analytics that can't just query the database.

1268
01:27:15.315 --> 01:27:26.601
Keep them in check by delaying sharding, choosing a key that makes the main paths single-shard, pushing everything else to read models and a warehouse, and automating moves and migrations.

1269
01:27:27.951 --> 01:27:28.847
Part 9.

1270
01:27:29.127 --> 01:27:30.535
Summary and Practice.

1271
01:27:31.785 --> 01:27:32.873
Key takeaways.

1272
01:27:33.153 --> 01:27:36.139
Replication scales reads and survives failures.

1273
01:27:36.419 --> 01:27:38.489
Partitioning scales writes and storage.

1274
01:27:38.769 --> 01:27:40.262
Real systems do both.

1275
01:27:40.542 --> 01:27:44.275
Range keeps scans local but concentrates monotonic writes.

1276
01:27:44.555 --> 01:27:47.969
Hash spreads writes but scatters range and non-key queries.

1277
01:27:48.249 --> 01:27:53.027
Never let the shard count be part of a key's address. mod N moved 80 %.

1278
01:27:53.307 --> 01:28:01.201
Fixed buckets + a versioned map move ~1 or N (19.7 %), one verifiable bucket at a time.

1279
01:28:01.481 --> 01:28:08.969
Choose the key from the dominant queries and transactions, co-locate what's written together, and plan every other query explicitly.

1280
01:28:10.319 --> 01:28:14.351
Put the bucket in the id so by-id reads route with no lookup.

1281
01:28:14.631 --> 01:28:17.319
Unique constraints include the partition key.

1282
01:28:17.599 --> 01:28:20.351
Global uniqueness comes from the id generator.

1283
01:28:20.631 --> 01:28:25.687
Scatter-gather is bounded: deadlines, per-shard limits, partial results, off hot paths.

1284
01:28:25.967 --> 01:28:28.633
Use read models for frequent non-key queries.

1285
01:28:28.913 --> 01:28:30.684
Hashing doesn't fix a hot key.

1286
01:28:30.964 --> 01:28:36.212
Salt it (4.11x, then 1.68x), cache it, or isolate it.

1287
01:28:36.492 --> 01:28:42.892
Moves need a fence: copy while writable, catch up, fence, final delta, verify, flip, delete later.

1288
01:28:43.172 --> 01:28:45.263
Naive moves lost 88 writes.

1289
01:28:45.543 --> 01:28:47.143
Fenced moves lost 0.

1290
01:28:48.493 --> 01:28:50.903
Stale routing must fail safe and self-heal:

1291
01:28:51.183 --> 01:28:54.767
MOVED, ASK, BUCKETMOVED, map versions that only increase.

1292
01:28:56.117 --> 01:28:57.440
Decision cheat sheet.

1293
01:28:57.720 --> 01:28:59.064
Here is a quick table:

1294
01:28:59.344 --> 01:29:00.453
